    </header>
    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="9" relname="concession">Ideally errors would not occur, especially catastrophic errors.</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="9" relname="span">Error recovery is a multi-faceted usability attribute.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="12" relname="span">It is one of several aspects of the safety usability goal described by Preece (pg. 14-15).</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="3" relname="elaboration">Error recovery attempts to prevent users from carrying out unwanted actions.</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="6" relname="circumstance">Additionally, error recovery implies that when errors do occur,</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="13" relname="span">they are easily overcome by providing various means of recovery.</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="11" relname="volitional-result">Taken in this context it is easy to understand why Preece included error recovery as a component of the safety usability goal.</segment>
        <group id="8" type="span"/>
        <group id="9" type="span" parent="8" relname="span"/>
        <group id="10" type="span" parent="2" relname="elaboration"/>
        <group id="11" type="span" parent="10" relname="span"/>
        <group id="12" type="span" parent="11" relname="span"/>
        <group id="13" type="span" parent="12" relname="elaboration"/>
    </body>
</rst>
